    },
    {
      "family": "associated_nonvoting_records",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C31", "C32", "C37", "C38"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental skin sensitization study (human, LLNA, or guinea pig) for the exact query compound.",
    "Uncertainty whether the isothiazole S-oxide without the isothiazolinone carbonyl/N-methyl has the same reactivity and potency as MI/MBIT.",
    "No repeat-insult patch test or dose-response data for the query; potency cannot be estimated.",
    "The DNTB weak-sensitization context is only partially analogous and indicates that some activated aryl chlorides can be weak or inefficient sensitizers."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted risk: the query is a small lipophilic isothiazole S-oxide aryl chloride. Structurally related isothiazolinones (MI, MBIT, OIT, DCOIT) are validated extreme human/regulatory contact sensitizers, and the activated aryl chloride motif parallels DNCB sensitization. L2 records add further positive MI patch-test and DNCB sensitization evidence; the only weak/negative context (DNTB) is structurally distinct and does not outweigh the transferable positive evidence."
}

